---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout title="Research Philosophy">
  <h1 data-reveal>Research philosophy</h1>

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede" data-reveal>
      A lot of the things we care about can't be observed directly.
    </p>
    <p data-reveal>
      Whether the subject is trauma, child development, or neural networks, we're
      usually limited to indirect measurements — behavior, test responses, neural
      activity, model outputs. The scientific challenge is to recover the hidden process
      that generated those observations.
    </p>
    <p data-reveal>
      That question first appeared in my work on transgenerational trauma, where
      behavioral changes suggested epigenetic mechanisms that could not be measured
      directly. It resurfaced while developing a shortened developmental assessment,
      where latent cognitive abilities had to be inferred from hundreds of behavioral
      test items. Now it motivates my work in computational neuroscience, where I study
      how recurrent neural networks organize internal representations while solving
      adaptive decision-making tasks.
    </p>

    <h2 data-reveal>What kinds of questions excite me</h2>
    <p data-reveal>
      I enjoy problems where the observable behavior is simple but the underlying
      computation is not. I'm fascinated whenever multiple organisms or algorithms
      produce nearly identical outputs while relying on fundamentally different internal
      representations. Understanding those hidden computations, and learning how to
      recover them, is the motivation that connects most of my work.
    </p>

    <h2 data-reveal>Current questions</h2>
    <p data-reveal>
      How do recurrent neural networks organize their hidden-state geometry while
      solving sequential decision-making tasks?
    </p>
    <p data-reveal>
      Which analyses genuinely reveal computation — fixed points, basin structure,
      manifold geometry?
    </p>
    <p data-reveal>
      Can latent-state models such as GLM-HMMs recover meaningful computational
      strategies, or are they mostly descriptive?
    </p>
    <p data-reveal>
      How should we compare the internal computations of biological brains and
      artificial neural networks?
    </p>

    <h2 data-reveal>Open problems</h2>
    <p data-reveal>
      I'm interested in which latent representations stay consistent across
      independently trained networks. If several models solve the same task with similar
      behavior but different internal dynamics, what aspects of computation are truly
      fundamental? Longer term, I want to understand how these ideas scale from
      simplified behavioral tasks to richer neural population recordings and eventually
      to closed-loop neurotechnology.
    </p>

    <h2 data-reveal>Things I've changed my mind about</h2>
    <h3 data-reveal>Trusting beautiful visualizations too quickly</h3>
    <p data-reveal>
      While analyzing hidden-state manifolds, I assumed that visually compelling PCA
      trajectories reflected meaningful computation. Checking my analyses repeatedly
      taught me that attractive plots are easy to generate and surprisingly easy to
      misinterpret. I've become much more careful about validating every visualization
      against the underlying data and model assumptions.
    </p>
    <h3 data-reveal>Assuming that agreement implied identical computation</h3>
    <p data-reveal>
      Some of the most productive parts of my projects started with results that looked
      wrong. An unexpected transition matrix, a strange latent trajectory, or a model
      behaving differently than intuition predicted has often forced me to reconsider my
      assumptions. I treat surprising results as openings to understand the system more
      deeply rather than problems to make disappear.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
